go-style defer for c in a single header.


deferred code runs when the enclosing scope exits.
multiple defers run in reverse order.
gcc uses a nested function with a cleanup attribute.
clang uses a block and needs -fblocks.
on linux, clang also needs BlocksRuntime.


    #include "defer.h"
    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>
    #include <unistd.h>

    int main(void) {
        char *mem = malloc(100);
        defer({
            free(mem);
            printf("mem freed\n");
        });

        printf("mem allocated\n");
        return EXIT_SUCCESS;
    }

    $ make run
    mem allocated
    mem freed


make run only works on macos with homebrew clang.
the docker targets work everywhere.

    $ make build-image
    $ make docker-gcc-test
    $ make docker-clang-test


caveats:

- use one defer per line.
- use semicolons, not commas, inside a defer block.
- don't name a captured variable ptr.
- clang needs __block to modify captured variables.
- clang can't capture arrays, so capture a pointer.
- don't goto past a defer. gcc still runs it.
- longjmp, abort, _exit and signals skip defers.


references:

- c2y defer: https://thephd.dev/c2y-the-defer-technical-specification-its-time-go-go-go
- N2895: https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n2895.htm
- linux cleanup.h: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/include/linux/cleanup.h
- blocks-based defer: https://fdiv.net/2015/10/08/emulating-defer-c-clang-or-gccblocks
